    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Maass-Chemistry-Bldg/Assistant-Professor-in-Materials-Chemistry----Canada-Impact--Emerging-Leaders----Department-of-Chemistry_JR0000079819": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Materials Chemistry - (Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders) – Department of Chemistry",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Program Description The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in recruiting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders will receive research funding and institutional support to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas , build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world. The Department of Chemistry at McGill University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position to advance transformational research programs that support the growth of Canada’s global leadership in the following strategic priority area: Clean technology and resource value-chain Emerging Leaders must have an independent program of research that demonstrates strategic alignment with this priority area. The award provides $100,000 per year for six years to McGill University to support the salary costs associated with the successful Emerging Leader appointment as well as direct research expenses, with the possibility of extension for a further six years.  Successful candidates will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than June 29, 2027 . Job Duties The successful candidate will be expected to mount a vigorous, innovative research program in Materials Chemistry, in line with McGill University’s strategic commitment in Advanced Materials and Sustainability. This commitment has resulted in the recent acquisition of more than $30M of dedicated research infrastructure in the Department of Chemistry. Applications in Energy and/or Sustainability are of interest (e.g., new materials for photovoltaics and energy storage; sustainable production and recycling of materials; heterogeneous catalysts for CO2 conversion). Candidates emphasizing advanced materials characterization or combined synthesis/characterization research programs as well as candidates with a wide range of interdisciplinary training experience are particularly encouraged to apply. Successful candidates will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses, develop courses in their areas of specialization, supervise and mentor graduate students, and contribute to the intellectual life of the University. Qualifications and Education Requirements Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on June 29, 2026, are eligible. Candidates must be within five years of the date of their first independent research-related appointment, minus eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible. Research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently. Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical, and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical, and administrative leave, are not credited. A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself. Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program prior to applying.   Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply. Application Process Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill’s HR management system. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday . Refer to the How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates) job aid for instructions on how to apply. Required Documents The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB. Cover letter Curriculum vitae  Detailed research proposal Start up budget listing equipment need Teaching statement and dossier Contact information for at least three references  Job Details Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Posting Period Review of applications will start on October 10, 2026. Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to the Administrative Officer, Department of Chemistry ( administrative-officer.chemistry@mcgill.ca ). Commitment to Equity and Diversity Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-1010/Director--Building-Sustainment-Planning_JR0000079895": {
      "title": "Director, Building Sustainment Planning",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Facilities Management and Ancillary Services (FMAS) is the steward of McGill University’s physical campuses and infrastructure. It oversees the management of the University’s facilities portfolio in support of institutional strategic priorities and operational needs. FMAS comprises more than 400 employees organized under four functional pillars: Integrated Planning, Project Delivery, Operations, and Ancillary and Support Services. Reporting to the Senior Director, Integrated Planning, the incumbent is responsible for establishing, planning, organizing and directing the activities of the Building Sustainment sub-unit including its budget and financial activities. They provide leadership to managers and experts under their responsibilities in the strategic planning of budget, policies, procedures and processes. They ensure alignment with Facilities Management and Ancillary Services mission and strategic plan. Primary Responsibilities: Establish the strategic direction of the Building Sustainment sub-unit in collaboration with the Senior Director Integrated Planning and oversees the implementation of short- and long-term plans related to the function of building sustainment planning, asset management, capital renewal and investment planning including budget, policies, procedures and processes. Define and implement key performance indicators aligned with Facilities Management and Ancillary Services strategic plan and operations as related to Building Sustainment. Lead the evolution of the Building Sustainment sub-unit to ensure alignment with external drivers such as Horizon McGill or the University’s Priorities and Objectives. Direct a multidisciplinary team of experts in their day-to-day operations. Provide operational leadership regarding the sub-unit’s goals and objectives. Direct building and sustainment infrastructure strategic plans, including deferred maintenance, capital renewal, lifecycle management, asset performance, risk and resilience, supported by data-driven planning, and the University wide portfolio management process and governance for capital projects. Establish and direct annual operating and capital budgets of the sub-unit under their authority. Assess budgetary impacts resulting from organizational and/or operational changes in Facilities Management and Ancillary Services. Establish training needs and work with the responsible managers and subject matter experts in developing training programs or in scheduling outside resources to address training needs for the Building Sustainment sub-unit. In collaboration with the Senior Director Integrated Planning, evaluate the effectiveness of the Building Sustainment sub-unit’s business and operational models. Develop methods of improving and/or expanding these models while ensuring integration with other FMAS processes. Provide technical expertise and leadership as required to support the Senior Director, Integrated Planning’s role to guide the McGill community on the delivery of services related to Building Sustainment. Foster relationships with McGill’s stakeholders and external organizations related to the Building Sustainment mandate. Ensure that all the responsible sub-units’ work is completed in compliance with the University and the Government building and construction policies, rules and regulations. Direct and ensure, in conjunction with other Facilities Management and Ancillary Services units, that Building Sustainment guidelines and standards are implemented and adhered in all renovation and/or construction. In collaboration with other internal stakeholders, negotiate service agreements related to their sub-units. Represent the University with public authorities, partners and stakeholders on matters related to building sustainment, infrastructure and asset management Provide strategic guidance and recommendation to McGill senior leadership and governance bodies in support of institutional decision-making Lead and organize multidisciplinary teams, including external expertise as required, to support building sustainment and asset management initiatives. Ensure alignment of the University’s building sustainment priorities with applicable external planning frameworks and regulatory requirements. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: An undergraduate degree in Architecture, Urban Planning, Engineering, or a related field is preferred. Eight (8) years of related experience in facilities, planning, real estate, infrastructure, or an equivalent domain. MBA or post‑graduate studies in finance or a related field is an asset. Eight (8) years of experience supervising teams, including leadership of professional staff. Excellent organizational, leadership, and delegation skills, with a demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across diverse teams. Strong written, presentation, and oral communication skills in both English and French, with the ability to communicate effectively with internal stakeholders at all organizational levels as well as external partners and authorities. Technical expertise in facilities management, including knowledge of compliance obligations and regulatory frameworks. Team management skills and ability to communicate diplomatically and firmly. Superior persuasion and negotiation skills, including the ability to diagnose key issues and achieve consensus. Advanced problem‑solving and change‑management abilities, with demonstrated success leading organizational or process transformations. Work experience at a senior management level in an academic and corporate environment is a strong asset. Demonstrated ability to strategically prioritize and manage multiple tasks and projects in a dynamic and evolving environment. Demonstrated technical skills appropriate to facilities planning, asset management, and infrastructure strategy. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 8 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 10) $129,460.00 - $161,820.00 - $199,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-LOG4E - Project Management - Director or equivalent Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Senior Director, Integrated Planning Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-15 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
